{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And it was told to the king, etc. that the people fled, etc. and the heart turned, etc. toward the people.</b> One needs to understand His saying ״כִּי בָרַח״ (that [the people] fled) and not ״כִּי הָלַךְ״ (that [the people] went) — it would appear that they saw in them a sign of flight. Further, it is difficult that He said <b>וַיֵּהָפֵךְ</b> (and the heart turned), etc., which implies that they regretted the thing they had done — and the matter is astonishing: did they do it willingly? Were they not struck with great plagues until they were nearly destroyed and finished, and against their will they sent them? How could their heart turn over a thing they did not do willingly? And it is further difficult, their saying <b>מַה זֹּאת עָשִׂינוּ</b> (what is this we have done), etc. — as if protesting against themselves for doing something improper, when they did it only under compulsion. Why should a person complain about something he did under compulsion?" "And it is possible to say, on the way of their statement of blessed memory (Zohar II:45a), that ״הָעָם״ (the people) is the mixed multitude, as we wrote at the beginning of the parashah. And the meaning of the verse is thus: ״And it was told to the king of Egypt that the people fled״ — meaning, the people whom he sent along with Israel, to serve as his spies to bring Israel back — they have now fled and gone on their way. And to these the term flight applies, for without fleeing there is no rescue from Pharaoh's hand, since they are his people and his servants." "<b>And the heart of Pharaoh turned</b>, etc. — meaning, they regretted having sent them; meaning, the people, not Israel. <b>And they said: What is this we have done</b> — meaning, they are astonished at their own deed of sending the people. And the reason for the astonishment is <b>that we have sent Israel from serving us</b> — its meaning follows their statement of blessed memory (Yalkut Shimoni 208), that Pharaoh wrote a bill of emancipation for Israel and said to them: you are your own, you are free men. And His saying <b>שִׁלַּחְנוּ</b> (we sent) is in the language of a bill of divorce; <b>מֵעָבְדֵנוּ</b> (from serving us) — that they have no status as slaves. And even if it crossed their minds that they would return, he would no longer work them with slave labor, and since that is so the labor required for the king would revert upon the people, and from now on, since the people fled, who will labor? Also in this there is a hint to why they were not worried about the flight of the said people: when they would feel that the labor had grown heavy upon them, they would therefore immediately and at once prepare themselves to pursue. And behold, according to what I have explained, the pursuit was only concerning the people. And Scripture says that HaShem hardened the heart of Pharaoh and he pursued also after the children of Israel, and not after the people alone." "<b>Further,</b> it appears based on what I explained in parashat Bo (Exodus 10:7), that the reason they hardened their necks, Pharaoh and his servants, was their thinking that the word of HaShem in this manner was a cunning stratagem — and this would imply that He is not able to do all He desires, heaven forbid. And behold, on the day HaShem struck them with the plague of the firstborn and measured them out as dead corpses, they quaked and trembled and vindicated the matter, that the Elohim of Israel is omnipotent and able to destroy the world, all the more so Egypt. And according to this reasoning, they would be compelled to say that what HaShem said, ״a journey of three days,״ involves no cunning, heaven forbid, but speaks truth. And Pharaoh's proof, from the fact that they all go together, is no proof, for the reason stated in the words of Moshe (ibid.) ״for it is a feast to HaShem,״ etc. And this served them as support when they lent Israel their money and wealth, for it became established in their hearts that Israel would surely return to Egypt. Nor were they concerned that HaShem had done the thing in this manner in order that they would lend it, since they recognized that HaShem was able to compel them even on this, to give all their wealth. And all the more so according to their words of blessed memory (Shemot Rabbah 14), that they revealed their secrets during the days of darkness of Egypt and the Egyptians knew through this — if so, for this reason too they could have taken everything with no one watching." "And when the teller told Pharaoh, saying to him ״that the people fled,״ meaning — according to what had emerged in their conclusion based on the proofs, that from HaShem the matter was not an irrevocable departure — if so, the fact that Israel did not want to return, because they wanted to flee from Egypt and go on an irrevocable journey, and this was not from HaShem. Therefore <b>the heart of Pharaoh and his servants turned toward the people</b> — meaning, not concerning the one who let them go, for the one who let them go still retains the assumption that he let them go only for a time, but rather concerning what pertains to the people: why were they not concerned about them, that they would flee. And that is their saying <b>What is this we have done, that we have sent Israel from serving us</b> — meaning: even though we had to send them by the decree of their Elohim, even so, we should not have sent them in a manner whereby they could flee from serving us, for they could have sent with them a force of Egypt, its chariots and its horsemen, until they worship their Elohim in the wilderness and return to Egypt." "<b>Or</b> it may mean that through the very act of the informer who told him that they had fled, Pharaoh's heart turned against what they had concluded — that the Elohim of Israel is omnipotent. And behold, this is for you a clear sign that He is not able over all, from the fact that He acted with cunning. Say from now on, by way of a decisive proof, that there was, Heaven forbid, no power in Him to bring them out decisively, and it was necessary to steal their minds, and the verse was precise to say." "<b>Levav</b> — to say that two hearts were resolved in the matter, for although at the beginning, before he sent Israel, he had this thought — that there is no power in his hand to bring them out decisively, from what he asks in this manner — even so he had another heart that rejected this reasoning and said perhaps He does not want them except for a short while. But now this argument flew away, and both hearts agreed together toward the people, not to send them, for the other side was clarified among them, and they said: 'What is this evil deed we have done, that we sent them, etc.' — for in retrospect we see that He could not bring them out decisively for the reason we have written. Therefore 'וַיֶּאְסֹר אֶת רִכְבּוֹ' (Exodus 14:6)."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:28:09.570060476Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}